%[text] # Skull Stripping and SynthSeg in MATLAB
%[text] Use this live script **if the HD Brain Extraction or the SynthSeg extension doesn't work in 3D Slicer** (for example, if Slicer hangs or crashes). It does the same two steps in MATLAB, with one run of **SynthSeg 2.0**, and saves the files that the Slicer extensions make:
%[text] - **MRBT1stripped.nrrd**: the MRI with the skull removed (made by HD Brain Extraction in Slicer)
%[text] - **MRBT1SkullStrip.seg.nrrd**: a segmentation with one segment, "brain" (made by HD Brain Extraction in Slicer)
%[text] - **MRBT1stripped\_synthseg\_v2.seg.nrrd**: the segmentation of the brain into 32 structures (made by SynthSeg in Slicer)
%[text] - **MRBT1stripped\_volumes.csv**: the volume of each structure (made by SynthSeg in Slicer) \
%[text] You will use these files in Slicer and in **SLCR3\_Tumor\_Comparisons**. The two steps are described in [Skull Stripping](https://salcedoe.github.io/MtMdocs/slicer/MRTumorSegment/#skull-stripping) and [SynthSeg](https://salcedoe.github.io/MtMdocs/slicer/MRTumorSegment/#synthseg) in the Slicer exercise.
%[text] ### Which MRI do we start from?
%[text] - **If you already stripped the skull in Slicer**, save the skull-stripped volume (`MRBrainTumor1 stripped`) in your project folder as **MRBT1stripped.nrrd**. This script finds it, and only runs SynthSeg
%[text] - **If you didn't** (HD Brain Extraction didn't work), the script starts from **MRBrainTumor1**, and removes the skull too. SynthSeg doesn't need the skull removed, so one run of SynthSeg gives us both the brain structures and the brain mask \
%[text] ### Requirements
%[text] - The Deep Learning Toolbox, the Image Processing Toolbox, and the Medical Imaging Toolbox. You don't need Python or any support packages
%[text] - About 8 GB of memory. It takes about a minute. If your laptop only has 8 GB, makes sure you quit everything else. Also, make sure your MATLAB settings:Java Runtime Environment is set to "11" (Maxed out). 
%[text] - ![](text:image:2358)  \
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%%
%[text] ## Step 1: Choose your project folder
%[text] Click the button below and choose the folder where you save your Slicer files. Then run the section.
clearvars
close all
paths.project = "/Users/ernesto/Desktop/Tumors"; %[control:filebrowser:a7c1]{"position":[17,48]}
paths.stripped = fullfile(paths.project, "MRBT1stripped.nrrd"); % the skull-stripped MRI
paths.mask = fullfile(paths.project, "MRBT1SkullStrip.seg.nrrd"); % the brain segmentation
paths.synthseg = fullfile(paths.project, "MRBT1stripped_synthseg_v2.seg.nrrd"); % the SynthSeg segmentation
paths.volumes = fullfile(paths.project, "MRBT1stripped_volumes.csv"); % the SynthSeg volumes
isfolder(paths.project)
%[text] - If the answer is 0 (false), choose your project folder with the button \
%%
%[text] ### OPTIONAL: No project folder
%[text] If you don't have a project folder, uncomment and run this code. It saves the new files in a folder called **SynthSegTumor** in your MATLAB folder (**userpath**). (We can't save files in the course data folder.)
% paths.project = fullfile(userpath, "SynthSegTumor");
% if ~isfolder(paths.project), mkdir(paths.project), end
% paths.stripped = fullfile(paths.project, "MRBT1stripped.nrrd");
% paths.mask = fullfile(paths.project, "MRBT1SkullStrip.seg.nrrd");
% paths.synthseg = fullfile(paths.project, "MRBT1stripped_synthseg_v2.seg.nrrd");
% paths.volumes = fullfile(paths.project, "MRBT1stripped_volumes.csv");
%%
%[text] ## Step 2: Load the MRI
%[text] If your project folder has **MRBT1stripped.nrrd**, we use it. Otherwise, we use **MRBrainTumor1** from the course data folder (the same MRI that you loaded in Slicer), and remove the skull in Step 4:
warning("off", "medical:medicalVolume:noEndian") % Slicer's NRRD files trigger a harmless warning
hasStripped = isfile(paths.stripped)
if hasStripped
    paths.mri = paths.stripped;
    disp("Found MRBT1stripped.nrrd: we use your skull-stripped MRI")
else
    paths.mri = fullfile(mmSetUnitDataFolder(3), "Tumors", "MRBrainTumor1.nrrd");
    disp("No MRBT1stripped.nrrd: we start from MRBrainTumor1, and remove the skull in Step 4")
end
mriMV = medicalVolume(paths.mri)
%[text] - The voxels are not cubes: they are 0.94 mm wide, but 1.4 mm thick. SynthSeg works on 1 mm voxels, so it resamples the MRI first \
%%
%[text] ## Step 3: Run SynthSeg
%[text] The course function **mmSynthSeg2** runs SynthSeg 2.0. It needs the MRI, and the names of the two files to save:
[synthMV, synthT, volumes] = mmSynthSeg2(mriMV, OutputFile=paths.synthseg, VolumesFile=paths.volumes);
%[text] - This takes about a minute. The first time, the function also loads the network
%[text] - If the network file isn't in your course toolbox, the function downloads it (about 50 MB) and saves it in your MATLAB folder. This happens only once
%[text] - If the files already exist, they are replaced
%[text] - If you get an "out of memory" error, close other programs and try again. SynthSeg needs about 8 GB of memory \
%%
%[text] ### The outputs
%[text] **mmSynthSeg2** returns three outputs:
%[text] - **synthMV**: a medicalVolume with the label of each voxel
%[text] - **synthT**: a table with the name, label value, and color of each structure
%[text] - **volumes**: a table with the volume of each structure, in mm^3 (the same numbers as in the CSV file) \
synthT
%[text] - These are the same names and label values as in the Slicer extension, including **csf** (cerebrospinal fluid) \
%%
volumes(:, 1:6)
%[text] - **total intracranial** is the volume of everything inside the skull (the brain and the CSF), in mm^3. Divide by 1000 to convert to mL \
%%
%[text] ## Step 4: Remove the skull
%[text] If you started from MRBrainTumor1, we now remove the skull. The course function **mmSkullStrip** uses the SynthSeg labels that we already have:
%[text] 1. Every voxel that SynthSeg labeled (a brain structure or CSF) is part of the brain mask
%[text] 2. SynthSeg doesn't label the tumor, so the tumor is a hole in the mask. We fill the holes, so the tumor is inside the mask
%[text] 3. Every voxel outside the mask is set to 0 \
%[text] It saves the skull-stripped MRI and the brain mask in your project folder:
if hasStripped
    strippedMV = mriMV; % the skull is already gone
    disp("You already stripped the skull in Slicer, so we skip this step")
else
    [strippedMV, brainMV] = mmSkullStrip(mriMV, SynthSeg=synthMV, OutputFile=paths.stripped, MaskFile=paths.mask);
    brainVolume_mL = nnz(brainMV.Voxels) * prod(mriMV.VoxelSpacing) / 1000
end
%[text] - For MRBrainTumor1, this mask agrees closely with the HD Brain Extraction mask (Dice coefficient 0.97). It is a little larger, because it includes a thin layer of CSF just inside the skull \
%%
%[text] ## Step 5: Look at the results
%[text] SynthSeg works on 1 mm voxels, so its segmentation is on a different voxel grid than the MRI. To compare them voxel by voxel, we put the labels on the voxels of the MRI. We use nearest-neighbor interpolation, so that each voxel keeps a whole label (a label of 2.5 wouldn't mean anything):
labelMV = resample(synthMV, mriMV.VolumeGeometry, Method="nearest");
%[text] We display one transverse slice through the tumor: the MRI that we started from, the skull-stripped MRI, and the SynthSeg labels, with each structure in its color:
colors = zeros(max(synthT.LabelValue), 3);
colors(synthT.LabelValue, :) = synthT.Color; % row k = the color of label k
slice = 76; % a transverse slice through the middle of the tumor
figure
tiledlayout(1, 3, TileSpacing="tight")
nexttile
imshow(extractSlice(mriMV, slice, "transverse"), [])
title("Starting MRI")
nexttile
imshow(extractSlice(strippedMV, slice, "transverse"), [])
title("Skull stripped")
nexttile
imshow(labeloverlay(mat2gray(extractSlice(strippedMV, slice, "transverse")), ...
    extractSlice(labelMV, slice, "transverse"), Colormap=colors, Transparency=0.4))
title("SynthSeg")
%[text] - Change `slice` to look at other slices
%[text] - The white matter is white, the cortex is red, and the CSF is dark gray \
%%
%[text] ### Scroll through the slices
%[text] Open the skull-stripped MRI and the labels in the **Medical Volume Viewer** app:
medicalVolumeViewer(strippedMV, labelMV)
%[text] - The app numbers the labels instead of naming them. Look up each label value in `synthT`
%[text] - Scroll through the slices. Is there any segmentation at the tumors? Which structures did SynthSeg give the tumor voxels? \
%%
%[text] ## Step 6: Back to Slicer
%[text] The files are now in your project folder, with the names that Slicer and SLCR3 expect.
%[text] **If you started from MRBrainTumor1** (you didn't strip the skull in Slicer):
%[text] 1. Drag **MRBT1stripped.nrrd** and **MRBT1SkullStrip.seg.nrrd** into Slicer, and click **OK**. They open as the volume `MRBT1stripped` and the segmentation `MRBT1SkullStrip` (with the segment "brain"). Where the exercise says `MRBrainTumor1 stripped`, use `MRBT1stripped`
%[text] 2. Skip the SynthSeg section, and continue with [Save Your Files for MATLAB](https://salcedoe.github.io/MtMdocs/slicer/MRTumorSegment/#save-your-files-for-matlab). In **Merge the segmentations**, copy the tumors into `MRBT1SkullStrip` \
%[text] **If you started from MRBT1stripped.nrrd**: continue with [Save Your Files for MATLAB](https://salcedoe.github.io/MtMdocs/slicer/MRTumorSegment/#save-your-files-for-matlab).
%[text] Either way, the SynthSeg segmentation and the volumes file are already in your project folder, so you don't need to save them from Slicer. To see the SynthSeg segmentation in Slicer, drag **MRBT1stripped\_synthseg\_v2.seg.nrrd** into Slicer.
%[text] ### Citation
%[text] If you use SynthSeg in your own work, cite:
%[text] - Billot B et al. SynthSeg: Segmentation of brain MRI scans of any contrast and resolution without retraining. *Medical Image Analysis* 2023
%[text] - Billot B et al. Robust machine learning segmentation for large-scale analysis of heterogeneous clinical brain MRI datasets. *PNAS* 2023 \

